# HCS Linux core chroot packages (v2 plan §5 W1 — real graphical session)
#
# v1 shipped only the Wayland *client* libraries, so no compositor could ever
# start and the live ISO could only ever print a console banner. The compositor,
# the shell and the session plumbing are now part of the core set.

# --- Core Linux & Hardware Support -------------------------------------------
linux-image-amd64
live-boot
live-config
live-config-systemd
systemd
systemd-sysv
systemd-sysusers
udev
dbus
polkitd

# --- Graphics: GBM, Mesa renderers and Vulkan --------------------------------
#
# THE ENTIRE GRAPHICS STACK WAS MISSING UNTIL NOW.
#
# grep this file for mesa|vulkan|libgl|firmware|xf86 used to return nothing. The
# image shipped a Wayland compositor and no drivers for it, so niri started and
# then had nothing to talk to. "CPU-first" had been read as "no GPU needed" when
# the truth was "no GPU support was ever written".
#
# Three DIFFERENT things live here and must not be conflated:
#
#   1. DRM/GBM    — talking to the kernel's DRM device. libgbm1.
#   2. GL/EGL     — rendering. libgl1-mesa-dri (llvmpipe, iris, radeonsi, ...).
#   3. Vulkan      — vulkan ICDs. mesa-vulkan-drivers (lavapipe, iris, radov...).
#
# Installing mesa-vulkan-drivers alone is the classic trap: it gives a Vulkan ICD
# and still no GL, which presents as "drivers installed, nothing renders". libgbm1
# and libgl1-mesa-dri are separate packages and both are mandatory.
#
# llvmpipe is the guaranteed floor. It rasterises in the CPU and needs no GPU at
# all, which is what makes the CPU-first claim true rather than aspirational, and
# it is the only reason a machine with no supported graphics can reach a desktop.
libgbm1
libegl-mesa0
libgl1-mesa-dri
libglx-mesa0
libdrm2
libdrm-amdgpu1
libdrm-intel1
libdrm-nouveau2
libdrm-radeon1
# Mesa's Vulkan loader plus every Mesa ICD: lavapipe (CPU), iris (Intel),
# radv (AMD). The loader picks per actual hardware; the metapackage is not a
# universal driver and makes no promise about any specific GPU.
mesa-vulkan-drivers
# VA-API, for hcs-shot and any video work.
mesa-va-drivers
# Not a runtime dependency: vulkaninfo and glxinfo are how we PROVE a renderer
# exists. A graphics stack nobody can query is one nobody can verify.
vulkan-tools
mesa-utils
mesa-utils-bin
xvfb
x11-utils

# --- Firmware ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Without these, Wi-Fi and many GPUs come up silently with no output at all.
# On NVIDIA the display simply stays dark, with no diagnostic anywhere.
#
# LICENCE NOTE: non-free-firmware is a redistribution EXCEPTION, not a free
# software licence. It is named here, in the third-party notices and in the
# README. It is not buried in this list.
firmware-linux-free
non-free-firmware
firmware-iwlwifi
firmware-atheros
firmware-realtek
firmware-brcm80211

# --- X11 fallback: QA only ---------------------------------------------------
#
# NOT the product. HCS ships niri. This exists because VirtualBox's umugfx driver
# offers no usable mode (128x48) in some host configurations, and a legacy X
# stack may be the only thing that can present a frame there. Its purpose is to
# let the QA gate prove the image boots and draws; the compositor choice is
# secondary to that proof.
xserver-xorg-video-fbdev
x11-xserver-utils

# --- Display & Wayland (compositor + shell) ---------------------------------
#
# niri is NOT here: no Debian suite packages it. It is provisioned from
# config/package-lists/hcs-external.list by scripts/fetch_niri.sh, which builds
# it from niri's own hash-pinned release bundle. Putting it in this file would
# make `apt-get install` fail and would make verify_package_availability.py
# report a false accusation.
libwayland-client0
libwayland-server0
wayland-protocols
pipewire
pipewire-pulse
wireplumber
seatd
xdg-desktop-portal
xdg-desktop-portal-wlr
xdg-desktop-portal-gtk
gsettings-desktop-schemas
# quickshell ships in Debian sid, which is why the base is sid rather than
# trixie: 0.3.1 requires Qt 6.11.2 with an exact-version private ABI, and trixie
# has Qt 6.8. Pulling this one binary into trixie would drag the whole Qt 6.11
# stack in with it.
quickshell

# --- Boot splash -------------------------------------------------------------
plymouth
# The spinner theme ships inside plymouth-themes; there is no separate
# plymouth-theme-spinner package in Debian.
plymouth-themes

# --- Input, seat & clipboard -------------------------------------------------
xkb-data
x11-xkb-utils
wl-clipboard
wtype

# --- Fonts: the software renderer needs real fonts or text renders as boxes --
fonts-dejavu-core
fonts-dejavu
fonts-noto-core
fonts-noto-cjk
fonts-noto-color-emoji
fontconfig

# --- Locales: UI language is independent of the keyboard layout --------------
locales
locales-all
xdg-utils
keyboard-configuration

# --- Storage & Core Libraries ------------------------------------------------
sqlite3
libsqlite3-0
zram-tools
cryptsetup
btrfs-progs
calamares

# --- Networking & Basic Utilities -------------------------------------------
network-manager
wpasupplicant
curl
iproute2
sudo
bash-completion
nano
htop
tmux
git
less
tree
unzip
tar
zstd
ca-certificates

# --- Screenshot / screen record (hcs-shot backend) ---------------------------
grim
slurp
wl-clipboard
ffmpeg

# --- QA agent support -------------------------------------------------------
# The in-guest QA runner judges its own captures and reads text out of them.
# Without tesseract every `assert_text` step in the VM suite would have to be
# reported as "cannot be evaluated", which is not the same as passing.
tesseract-ocr
tesseract-ocr-eng
tesseract-ocr-deu
# The evidence disk handed back to the VirtualBox host is formatted by the
# guest, because the host attaches it as a raw blank volume.
dosfstools

# --- Fonts for terminal rendering -------------------------------------------
fonts-jetbrains-mono

# --- Snapshot / rollback (Mint + Omarchy pattern) ----------------------------
# btrfs-progs above provides the tools; the subvolume setup runs in the installer.
